[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is AltSendMe, and it\'s basically AirDrop for every device you own. Dropping a file, any file, any size, and it goes straight from your device to someone else\'s with no cloud, no account, and no upload then download wait.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everything is end-to-end encrypted, it\'s completely free, and it even runs right inside your phone\'s web browser. There\'s no file size limit at all, and there\'s one clever networking trick that makes two devices on opposite sides of the'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'planet connect directly. Stick around because that trick is the whole reason this thing works. AltSendMe comes from Tony Anthony, a developer based in Bengaluru. This is very much an independent, community-driven project,'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not a startup trying to sell you storage later. That framing matters because the entire pitch here is that your files never touch a company\'s servers in the first place. When the whole point is privacy, it helps to know it\'s built by'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'someone with nothing to upsell you, and people notice fast. The first commit landed in October 2025, and it\'s already past 8,400 GitHub stars. Think about why a simple file sender'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'blew up like that. Every free option people reach for caps you at a couple of gigabytes or hides the rest behind a subscription or quietly parks your private files on someone else\'s cloud. AltSendMe throws all three of those'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'problems out at once for nothing. That combination is exactly what struck a nerve. So, how do you actually use it? It\'s three steps. You drop your files or a whole folder into AltSendMe and it hands you a short, secure ticket. You'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'send that ticket to the other person however you like, a chat message, an email, a text. They paste it into their AltSendMe, and the transfer starts flowing directly between the two of you. No account sign up, no waiting for an'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'upload to finish first. The moment they paste the ticket, your files are already on the move. Here\'s the part I love most. You don\'t even need to install anything. This is AltSendMe running in a mobile web browser. The web version,'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is brand new, lets you send and receive files straight from a browser tab on your phone. Same encrypted direct transfer, nothing to download, nothing to sign in to. Open the page, drop a file, share the ticket, done. It turns'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any phone into an instant private drop point. Now, that clever trick I promised. The old way of sending a big file is painful. You upload the whole thing to a cloud service, wait, get a link, and'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hope the other person downloads it before it expires. Alt Send Me skips the middleman entirely. It uses a technique called hole punching to open a direct path between two devices even when both are hidden behind home routers and'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'firewalls. And if a direct link truly can\'t be made, it falls back to an encrypted relay that only ever sees scrambled data. Either way, your files go straight across and the transfer even resumes if your connection drops. And this isn\'t a lonely little side project'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'either. In well under a year, it\'s pulled in more than 8,400 stars, over 500 forks, and dozens of contributors, with new releases landing almost every week. It runs on Windows, Mac, Linux,'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Android, the command line, and the browser, all from one code base. For a tool you\'re trusting with your private files, that pace and that reach are exactly the signals you want. A few honest notes before you dive in. First,'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is true peer-to-peer, so for a direct transfer, both people generally need to be online at the same time. It\'s a live handoff, not a mailbox that holds your file for later. Second, it\'s young, first released in late 2025, so expect'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it to keep changing fast. And third, it\'s licensed under the AGPL, which is fantastic for keeping it free and open, but it\'s a strong copyleft license, so read it before you build a product on top of it. For just sending'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your own files, none of that gets in your way. If you\'re tired of upload bars, size caps, and handing your private files to yet another cloud, Alt Send Me is worth 5 minutes of your time. Try the web version right from your'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser, or grab the desktop and mobile apps. The repository and the website are both linked in the description. If this saved you from a cloud subscription, subscribe here because we dig up a new open source gem exactly like this every'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single week.'
